---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Specialty Protein",
    tags: [
      "best protein chips",
      "high protein chips",
      "protein crisps",
      "protein puffs"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Buyer's Guide"} badge={"Label-by-Label Ranking"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Best Protein Chips</Fragment>
      Protein chips promise the crunch of a bag of Doritos with the protein of a shake. A few come close. The rest deliver half the protein, more salt than you would guess, and a price per gram that makes a tub of whey look like a bargain.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#what-is-a-protein-chip">What a Protein Chip Is</a>
      <a href="#how-we-ranked">How We Ranked Them</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Our Top Picks</a>
      <a href="#side-by-side">The Numbers Side by Side</a>
      <a href="#protein-per-calorie">Protein per Bag and per 100 kcal</a>
      <a href="#sodium">Sodium: The Number to Check</a>
      <a href="#protein-source">Milk Isolate, Chicken or Plants</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g of Protein</a>
      <a href="#vs-whole-foods">Chips vs Jerky, Chickpeas and Edamame</a>
      <a href="#buying-on-amazon">Buying on Amazon</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          A protein chip is a salty snack built on a protein isolate instead of corn or potato, and the label is where the good ones separate from the marketing. The numbers that matter are protein per bag, protein per 100 calories, sodium, what the protein actually is, and what you pay for every 25 g of it. This guide ranks the protein chips and crisps you can buy on Amazon on exactly those five things, and then sets them against the snacks people usually reach for instead: beef jerky, roasted chickpeas and dry-roasted edamame. Label figures are per the label at the time of writing and vary by flavor, so check the bag you actually buy.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="what-is-a-protein-chip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What a Protein Chip Actually Is
          </h2>

          <p>
            A regular tortilla chip is mostly corn and oil, and a potato chip is mostly potato and oil. A protein chip replaces most of that starch with a protein isolate, usually milk protein isolate and whey protein isolate, and binds it with a little starch or fiber so it can be baked, popped or fried into something that crunches. The result is a snack with three or four times the protein of a normal chip and a fraction of the carbohydrate, which is why the category grew out of the low-carb and gym crowds before reaching supermarket shelves.
          </p>

          <p>
            Not every product sold as a protein chip works that way. Some, like Pure Protein's popped crisps or the newer protein versions of mainstream brands, start from a normal chip recipe and add some plant protein. Those land around 9 to 12 g of protein per serving instead of 18 to 20 g, and they keep more of the carbohydrate. Others, like Wilde, use chicken breast and egg whites as the base. All three approaches are legitimate; they simply give you very different numbers, and the front of the bag does not tell you which one you are buying.
          </p>

          <p>
            The useful way to think about a protein chip is as a better salty snack, not as a protein supplement. Even the best bag here delivers about 19 or 20 g of protein, roughly one scoop of whey, but at three to four times the price per gram and with more sodium and fat than a shake. If you need to hit a daily protein target cheaply, a tub of powder does that job better; our guide to <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein you need</a> explains how much you actually need. Where chips earn their place is in replacing a bag of ordinary chips you were going to eat anyway.
          </p>

          <p>
            That framing also explains why this guide cares so much about sodium. A salty snack is salty by design, and a protein chip that replaces one bag of regular chips is not a problem. The problem comes when people treat them as a health food and eat two or three bags a day on top of everything else, because the sodium adds up quickly. Protein chips are a tool for the craving, and the best ones are the ones that satisfy it with the most protein and the least collateral.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, protein chips sit in a crowded snack aisle next to bars, cookies and puffs. If you are comparing formats, our guide to the <a href="/best-protein-bars/">best protein bars</a> covers the sweet side of the same idea, and the companion guide to <a href="/best-protein-cookies/">protein cookies</a> covers the dessert end. Chips are the savory option, and on protein per calorie the best of them beat most bars.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-ranked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How We Ranked Them
          </h2>

          <p>
            We started with every protein chip, crisp and puff we could confirm was sold on Amazon US in November 2026, then read the nutrition panel and ingredient list for each, using the brand's own label where it was published and grocery retailer labels where it was not. Where sources disagreed, which happens often in this category because brands reformulate and resize bags, we give a range and say so. None of these products was tested hands-on; taste and texture notes reflect what buyers report.
          </p>

          <p>
            The first measure is protein per bag, because that is what you actually eat. A snack-size bag is the natural portion, and a chip with 20 g in a 1.2 oz bag is more useful than one that quotes 10 g per serving in a family-size bag you will not stop at one serving of. The second is protein per 100 calories, which tells you how much of what you eat is protein rather than oil and starch. The best dairy chips here land around 12.5 to 14 g per 100 calories; ordinary tortilla chips land around 1.5.
          </p>

          <p>
            The third measure is sodium, both per bag and per 10 g of protein. The second number matters more than it looks: two chips with the same sodium per bag are not equal if one delivers twice the protein. The fourth is the protein source. Milk and whey isolates are complete proteins with plenty of leucine; chicken and egg are complete too; plant blends vary, and a product that does not say which plant proteins it uses gets less credit.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fifth measure is cost per 25 g of protein, the same yardstick we use for powders across the site, so you can compare a bag of chips with a scoop directly. We used prices we saw in November 2026 on Amazon, the brand's own store or major retailers, and we say which one. Prices move constantly in this category, especially for multipacks, so treat these as a guide to relative value rather than a quote.
          </p>

          <p>
            We left out products we could not confirm were sold on Amazon, which is why Doritos Protein, the most talked-about launch of 2026, does not get a card here. Its label is interesting, and we cover it in the comparison below, but at the time of writing it was easier to find at warehouse clubs and supermarkets than through a listing sold by Amazon.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Best Protein Chips, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            Each pick was chosen on its label rather than on taste testing: protein per bag and per 100 calories, sodium, the protein source and cost per 25 g of protein at prices we saw in November 2026. Nutrition figures are per the label at the time of writing and vary by flavor; prices move constantly, so check the live listing and buy from a listing that ships from and is sold by Amazon or from the brand's own storefront. Taste comments reflect what buyers report, not hands-on testing.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="side-by-side" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Numbers Side by Side
          </h2>

          <p>
            Here is every pick on one table, per bag, per the label at the time of writing. Where retailer labels disagree we show a range. The last column is the cost per 25 g of protein at the prices noted in each product card, and it moves with every sale, so use it to compare products rather than to budget.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Chip</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein / bag</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Calories</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein per 100 kcal</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sodium / bag</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Cost per 25 g</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Quest Tortilla Style (Loaded Taco)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">19 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">140</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">13.6 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">300 mg</td>
