          </div>

          <p>
            Three groups jump out. The dairy-isolate snacks from Twin Peaks, Quest and Legendary deliver about 12.5 to 17 g of protein per 100 calories, which is as good as or better than most protein bars. Pure Protein's crisps sit in the middle at roughly 8 to 10 g per 100 calories, because a larger share of the bag is still starch. Wilde's chicken chips trail on this measure at about 6 g, because they are fried and carry more fat, even though the protein itself is excellent.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sodium is less spread out than protein. Every pick lands between about 260 and 430 mg per bag or serving, which is roughly 11 to 19 percent of the 2,300 mg daily value. The difference is in what you get for it: Quest Loaded Taco delivers 10 g of protein for about 160 mg of sodium and Twin Peaks for about 190 mg, while Wilde needs about 260 mg and Pure Protein about 275 mg for the same 10 g.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cost, at the Amazon prices we saw in November 2026, rewards the same chips. Quest's 12-count boxes sold by Amazon land at about $2.95 to $3.10 per 25 g, and Twin Peaks at about $3.20 at Target and Vitacost prices. The two listings sold by third-party sellers on Amazon, Pure Protein and Wilde, come out at roughly $6.50 to $7.35 per 25 g, because a reseller's price on a modest-protein bag adds up fast. None of them comes close to the cost of protein from a tub of whey, which typically runs about $1 to $1.50 per 25 g.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="protein-per-calorie" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Protein per Bag and per 100 Calories
          </h2>

          <p>
            Protein per bag is the headline number, and it sorts the field into two tiers. Quest and Legendary deliver 19 to 20 g in a single bag, a genuine meal-sized dose of protein. Twin Peaks matches that in a 30 g serving of puffs, though it comes from a 10-serving bag. Wilde and Pure Protein deliver 12 to 13 g, which is still far more than a normal chip but closer to a cheese stick or a couple of eggs. If you are reaching for a chip to cover a gap after training or between meals, the first tier is the one that moves the needle.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protein per 100 calories tells you the efficiency of that protein, and here Twin Peaks pulls ahead: 21 g of protein for about 120 to 130 calories works out to roughly 16 to 17 g per 100 calories. Quest follows at about 13.6, Legendary at about 12.5. Those figures are in the same range as a lean protein bar and well above most whole-food snacks. For comparison, an ordinary bag of tortilla chips gives roughly 2 g of protein in 140 calories.
          </p>

          <p>
            The low-efficiency chips are low for different reasons. Pure Protein's crisps keep more starch from the base recipe, so about 13 g of carbohydrate shares the bag with 12 g of protein. Wilde's chips are low on carbohydrate but higher in fat from frying, at around 210 calories per 1.34 oz bag. Neither is a bad snack, but if you are counting calories, a bag of Wilde costs you roughly twice the calories of a Quest bag for less protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fat and carbohydrate round out the picture. The dairy chips land at about 5 to 8 g of fat and 4 to 5 g of carbohydrate per bag, with 1 g of fiber and next to no sugar, which is why they suit low-carb eaters. Our guide to <a href="/best-low-carb-protein-powder/">low-carb protein powder</a> covers the same macros in powder form. The trade-off is that a protein chip with almost no carbohydrate is less filling per calorie than you might expect; it is protein and oil, not fiber.
          </p>

          <p>
            A practical rule: if a protein chip delivers less than about 10 g of protein per 100 calories, think of it as a better potato chip rather than a protein snack. Above 12 g per 100 calories, it can stand in for a protein bar. That rule puts Twin Peaks, Quest and Legendary on one side and Pure Protein and Wilde on the other, and it is the single most useful comparison on the back of the bag.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sodium" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Sodium: The Number Nobody Checks
          </h2>

          <p>
            Protein chips are seasoned like chips, and that means salt. Every pick here sits between about 260 and 430 mg of sodium per bag or serving, and most are in the 300s. On its own that is a moderate amount, and a single bag fits comfortably within the 2,300 mg daily value most adults are advised to stay under. The problem is that protein chips invite repetition in a way that a shake does not.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sodium per 10 g of protein is the fairer comparison, because it tells you how much salt comes along with the reason you bought the bag. Quest Loaded Taco lands at about 160 mg per 10 g, Quest Sour Cream & Onion at about 180, Twin Peaks at about 185 to 195, Legendary at about 210, Wilde at about 260 and Pure Protein at about 275. If you eat protein chips daily, the Quest tortilla range gives you the most protein for the least salt, and flavor matters: within one brand, plain and taco-style flavors usually run lower than cheese and vinegar flavors.
          </p>

          <p>
            For people with high blood pressure, kidney disease or heart failure, sodium is not a footnote. If your doctor has given you a sodium limit, a protein chip should count against it like any other salty snack, and two bags of Legendary can use up more than a third of a 2,300 mg day by themselves. Our guide to <a href="/best-low-sodium-protein-powder/">low-sodium protein powder</a> covers protein options that keep sodium in double digits per serving, which is a better daily base. This is not medical advice; if you have a kidney or heart condition, ask your doctor or a registered dietitian how protein snacks fit your plan.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is also a quieter sodium source in the ingredient lists: caseinates. Sodium caseinate, one of the protein ingredients in Legendary's blend, contributes some sodium before any seasoning goes on, which is part of why dairy chips built on caseinates tend to read higher. That is not a reason to avoid them, just a reason the numbers differ even between chips that taste similarly salty.
          </p>

          <p>
            The simplest habit is to read the sodium line before the protein line. If a bag is above about 400 mg, make it the salty snack for the day rather than one of several. If you want a crunchy protein snack with almost no sodium, unsalted dry-roasted edamame or plain nuts do that job better than any protein chip currently sold, as the comparison section below shows.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="protein-source" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Milk Isolate, Chicken or Plants
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most protein chips are built on dairy, and that is a good thing. Milk protein isolate and whey protein isolate are complete proteins with a high share of leucine, the amino acid most closely tied to muscle protein synthesis. Quest lists both first in its chips, Twin Peaks builds its puffs on milk protein isolate, and Legendary uses a mix of caseinates and isolates. If your goal is muscle, these chips deliver protein of the same quality as a whey shake, just in a crunchier and pricier format.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wilde is the exception and the reason it earns a place despite weak efficiency numbers. Its chips start with antibiotic-free chicken breast, then add egg whites and chicken bone broth, with tapioca starch to make the chip. Chicken and egg are complete proteins too, and the result contains no milk, which makes it the obvious choice for people with a dairy allergy or anyone avoiding milk. If dairy is your issue in powder as well, our guide to <a href="/best-dairy-free-protein-powder/">dairy-free protein powder</a> covers the shake side.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plant-based crisps are the hardest to judge from the outside. Pure Protein describes its popped crisps as made with plant-based protein, and the listings we could read flag soy as an allergen, but they do not name the protein ingredients in full. PopCorners' newer protein line, by contrast, lists pea protein isolate, rice protein isolate and whey, which means it is not actually plant-only. A plant protein blend can be perfectly good, but a product that does not tell you what it is cannot be compared fairly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch for allergens across the category. Quest's chips contain milk and soy, Legendary's contain milk and egg, Wilde's contain egg, and Pure Protein's listings mention soy. Several are made in facilities that also handle wheat. All of the picks are marketed as gluten-free, but if you have celiac disease or a serious allergy, read the current bag rather than relying on an old listing, because snack brands reformulate frequently.
          </p>

          <p>
            One point often missed: protein chips are not protein-only foods. They contain oil, seasoning, starch and sometimes sugar or stevia. Quest lists stevia in its seasoning, and Legendary lists a little sugar and brown sugar. None of these are large amounts, but if you avoid stevia or added sugar entirely, the ingredient list matters as much as the panel.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Cost per 25 g of Protein
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cost per 25 g of protein is where protein chips look worst, and it is worth being honest about. On Amazon, the Quest Loaded Taco 12-count was about $26.98 and the Original Style Sour Cream & Onion 12-count about $28.47 when we checked (November 2026), both sold by Amazon.com; prices move. That is about $2.25 to $2.37 a bag, or about $2.95 to $3.10 per 25 g. Quest's own site charges more, $24.99 for 8 bags or about $4.10 per 25 g. Legendary was about $36.99 on Amazon from Legendary Foods' own listing, and its brand-site 7-pack at $24.99 lands at about $4.45 per 25 g.
          </p>

          <p>
            Third-party listings are where cost per gram falls apart. The Pure Protein variety pack was about $37.50 on Amazon from a third-party seller when we checked, which for 12 bags of 12 g is about $6.50 per 25 g. The Wilde Korean Sweet & Spicy 12-pack was about $45.87 from a third-party seller, roughly $7.35 per 25 g. Twin Peaks puffs, at about $26.99 for a 10-serving bag at Target and Vitacost, come to about $3.20 per 25 g; check the price on the brand's Amazon storefront before you buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Compare that with powder. A mainstream tub of whey typically delivers 25 g of protein for about $1 to $1.50, and our guide to <a href="/best-cheap-protein-powder/">cheap protein powder</a> shows several under that. Even the cheapest protein chip here, Quest on Amazon at about $2.95 per 25 g, costs roughly two to three times as much per gram, and the third-party listings cost five times as much or more. What you pay for is crunch, seasoning and a format that feels like a treat, which is a reasonable thing to buy as long as you know that is the deal.
          </p>

          <p>
            Subscribe & Save and multipacks are where the savings live. Quest's own subscription drops the 8-count to $21.24, and Amazon's Subscribe & Save discount applies to many chip listings sold by Amazon. Buying a single bag at a convenience store is the most expensive way to eat any of these. Our <a href="/amazon-protein-powder/">Amazon buying guide</a> explains how Subscribe & Save works and how to spot when a discount has quietly shrunk.
          </p>

          <p>
            If cost per gram is your main concern, the best approach is a split: get most of your protein from food and a cheap powder, and keep protein chips for the moments when you would otherwise buy ordinary chips. Used that way, the premium is small, because you are comparing a $2 to $3 protein chip with a $2 bag of regular chips, not with a scoop of whey.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-whole-foods" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Protein Chips vs Jerky, Chickpeas and Edamame
          </h2>

          <p>
            Protein chips compete with savory snacks that already contain protein, and the comparison is not always flattering. Beef jerky is the closest rival. USDA data for beef jerky puts a 1 oz portion at roughly 9 to 10 g of protein and about 115 calories, which is about 8 g of protein per 100 calories. That is a good ratio, but jerky typically carries roughly 500 to 600 mg of sodium per ounce, more than any chip here, and premium brands cost as much per gram as Quest.
          </p>

          <p>
            Roasted chickpeas are the high-fiber alternative. A typical 1 oz serving of crunchy roasted chickpeas provides about 5 to 6 g of protein, 120 to 130 calories and several grams of fiber, depending on the brand and the oil used. That is under 5 g of protein per 100 calories, about a third of a Quest chip, but you get fiber the protein chips mostly lack, and chickpeas are far more filling per calorie. They are a better snack for satiety and a worse one for protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dry-roasted edamame is the surprise winner on paper. A typical 1 oz (about a quarter cup) serving provides about 13 to 14 g of protein for around 130 calories, roughly 10 g per 100 calories, along with fiber, and lightly salted versions often come in well under 200 mg of sodium. It is complete soy protein, it is cheap, and it costs less per 25 g than any protein chip here. If you want a crunchy, salty, high-protein snack and do not need it to taste like a nacho chip, edamame is hard to beat.
          </p>

          <p>
            The newer mainstream protein chips are worth a mention too. Doritos Protein, launched in 2026, lists 10 g of protein per 1 oz serving for 150 calories and 150 mg of sodium per retailer labels, which is about 6.7 g of protein per 100 calories with low sodium. PopCorners' protein line lists 9 g of protein per 1 oz serving for 140 calories. Both are better than ordinary chips, but both land well below the dairy isolate chips on protein per calorie.
          </p>

          <p>
            So where do protein chips win? They win when you want the specific experience of a seasoned chip, with 18 to 20 g of complete protein and almost no carbohydrate, in a sealed bag that lasts in a drawer. Edamame wins on cost and sodium, jerky on portability for long days, chickpeas on fiber. Many people will do best keeping a box of Quest or Legendary for cravings and edamame for everyday snacking.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying-on-amazon" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Buying Protein Chips on Amazon
          </h2>

          <p>
            Amazon is the easiest place to buy protein chips in bulk, and it is also where most of the pricing confusion happens. The same Quest flavor can appear in a 12-count from Amazon, an 8-count from a third-party seller, and a bundle with protein cookies, all at different prices per bag. Before you compare, divide the price by the number of bags and then by the protein per bag; the box count and bag size vary more than the headline price suggests.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy from listings that say ships from and sold by Amazon, or from the brand's own storefront. Snack foods have expiry dates, and third-party sellers sometimes move older stock or ship boxes that have sat in hot warehouses, which matters for chips that can go stale or rancid. Check the expiry date when the box arrives and use Amazon's return process if it is close. Our <a href="/amazon-protein-powder/">guide to buying protein on Amazon</a> covers how to spot third-party listings and fakes in more detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stock is a real issue in this category. Some protein puff and PopCorners protein listings have shown as currently unavailable at times, and flavor ranges change often. If a favorite flavor disappears, check the brand's site before paying a reseller's premium; the brand may simply have renamed it or moved it into a variety pack. Variety packs are also the cheapest way to find a flavor you like before committing to a single-flavor box.
          </p>

          <p>
            Subscribe & Save makes sense once you know what you like. Discounts of 5 to 15 percent on a $25 to $35 box add up over a year, and a monthly delivery stops you from paying convenience-store prices. Just set the delivery interval to how many you actually eat; a 12-count every month only makes sense if you eat about three bags a week.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, store the bags somewhere cool and dry, keep an eye on the best-by date, and do not assume the formula you bought last year is the one in the current box. Quest, Legendary and Wilde have all changed bag sizes or labels over time. Reading the panel takes ten seconds and is the only way to know the protein and sodium you are actually getting.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating protein chips as a protein supplement</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Even the best bag delivers about one scoop's worth of protein at three or four times the price per gram, with more sodium and fat than a shake. Use protein chips to replace a bag of regular chips you would eat anyway, and get most of your daily protein from food and a cheaper powder instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing chips on the front-label protein number</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A bag that says 10 g per serving may be a family-size bag with several servings, while another lists 20 g in a single bag. Compare protein per bag you will actually eat, then protein per 100 calories, which shows how much of the snack is protein rather than oil and starch.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring sodium because the bag says healthy</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most protein chips carry 300 to 430 mg of sodium per bag, and two or three bags a day can use up a large share of the 2,300 mg daily value. Check sodium per bag and per 10 g of protein, and favor lower-sodium flavors if you snack on them daily.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming every protein chip uses whey</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Some chips use milk and whey isolates, some use chicken and egg, and some use plant blends that the listing does not name in full. If you need complete protein, have a dairy or soy allergy, or avoid animal products, read the ingredient list rather than assuming from the brand name.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying single bags or third-party boxes</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Single bags at convenience stores cost the most per gram, and third-party Amazon sellers sometimes ship stock close to its best-by date. Buy multipacks that ship from and are sold by Amazon or the brand, compare price per bag, and use Subscribe & Save once you settle on a flavor.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What are the best protein chips?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  On the label, Quest Tortilla Style Protein Chips are the best all-round pick: about 19 g of milk and whey isolate protein for 140 calories, with 300 mg of sodium in Loaded Taco, per the label at the time of writing, and about $2.95 per 25 g on Amazon when we checked. Twin Peaks Protein Puffs win on protein per calorie, Legendary on protein per bag, and Wilde is the pick if you need a dairy-free chip.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Are protein chips healthy?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  They are a better salty snack than ordinary chips, with far more protein and fewer carbohydrates, but they are still processed snacks with oil, seasoning and roughly 260 to 430 mg of sodium per bag. One bag a day fits easily into most diets. If you have high blood pressure or kidney disease, count them against your sodium limit and ask your doctor or a dietitian.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Which protein chips have the most protein?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Per bag, Legendary Foods Popped Protein Chips list 20 g and Quest's tortilla and original style chips list 18 to 19 g, per the labels at the time of writing. Twin Peaks Protein Puffs also list 21 g per 30 g serving and lead per 100 calories at about 16 to 17 g. Pure Protein and Wilde deliver about 12 to 13 g per bag.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Are protein chips good for weight loss?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  They can help if they replace higher-calorie snacks, because the dairy chips deliver around 13 g of protein per 100 calories and protein is more filling than starch. They will not help if they are added on top of your usual snacks. Choose by protein per calorie: Twin Peaks and Quest lead, while Wilde carries about twice the calories per gram of protein.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Do protein chips have a lot of sodium?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Most carry 300 to 430 mg per bag, about 13 to 19 percent of the daily value, which is similar to regular chips per bag. The fairer test is sodium per 10 g of protein: Quest Loaded Taco runs about 160 mg, while Wilde and Pure Protein run about 260 to 275 mg. Unsalted edamame or nuts are better if you need very low sodium.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Are protein chips keto-friendly?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  The dairy-based chips are low in carbohydrate: Quest lists about 5 g of total carbohydrate and 1 g of fiber per bag, and Legendary lists about 4 g of net carbs. Wilde's chicken chips are also low-carb. Pure Protein's popped crisps carry about 13 g of carbohydrate per bag, so they fit a moderate low-carb diet better than a strict keto one.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Are protein chips better than beef jerky?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  On protein per calorie, the dairy isolate chips beat jerky, at about 13 g per 100 calories against about 8 g for typical beef jerky, and they usually carry less sodium. Jerky is a whole-food protein and travels well for long days. Dry-roasted edamame beats both on cost and sodium, and roasted chickpeas win on fiber but deliver far less protein.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
        </div>

